            <div xml:id="d376">

                <pb facs="0530" n="448" xml:id="pg448"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.6176 F 51/51: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hood</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-07-23T16:00:00-00:44"><hi rendition="#italic">July 23, 1925—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received July 25—4:43
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>29. Department’s telegram 19, July 15, 6 p.m. President King feels that he must
                    have Secretary Barclay’s report and the actual signed documents in his hands
                    before issuing the call for a special session of the Legislature. There has been
                    a certain amount of adverse propaganda and President King desires to be in a
                    position to support all the loan terms unequivocally and to stand solidly for
                    immediate ratification. Upon receipt of the above information he will call a
                    special session, no matter how near the date of the regular session may be.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hood</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
